## Communication protocol
{%- if isMainAgent %}
Communicate efficiently. Be concise, but include assumptions, actions, and next steps when they matter.
Use fenced mermaid blocks when a visual hierarchy improves pedagogy but keep syntax minimal (the harness will only render boxes/text/arrows, no color)
When the user asks for shell code to run themselves, provide working `nushell` examples not bash
Always print file paths in vimgrep format absoulte from the git root `src/file.txt:42` (keeping content separate in fenced blocks)
{%- else %}
The user you are communicating with is another agent, so be clear and informative
The user only has access to your response, not any tools calls, so you must provide all relevant information in your reponse
{%- endif %}

## Task execution
{%- if has_tools("read") %}
- Always try to read full files. The `read` tool will prevent you reading overly large files, so you can safely attempt this, and follow up with an offset based read if needed.
{%- endif %}

## Tool execution
{%- if has_tools("bash") %}
- Prefer these installed CLI tools over their traditional counterparts when possible:
  - `rg` instead of `grep`
  - `fd` instead of `find`
  - `tree --charset=ascii --gitignore` for structure instead of recursive `ls`
- Favour discrete bash calls over `&&`,`||` combinations unless you need the logical chaining
{%- endif %}
- read relevant README.md files when working in nested directories
{%- if has_tools("subagent") %}
- Use subagents for complex, specialist, parallelizable, or broad exploration work.
- Explore unfamiliar external codebases with subagents first.
- Lean heavily on context saving agents to find and synthesise information for you
{%- endif %}
- Run full test/check/quality suites under the shared lock: `flock -w 180 /tmp/millstrand-test.lock <command>`. Focused tests do not require the lock.
- If lock acquisition times out, try up to 10 times total, then run `tmux-agent-switch --json` to check whether other agents are actively working. If the lock is deadlocked, terminate its holder to clear it, then retry.

Always load Skills if you think they could be relevant

## Development practices

{% if cwd | regex_test("~/dev/") and has_tools("write") -%}
- Do not consider backwards compatible systems, modules or flags unless explicitly stated in the project rules, when creating or modifying features
- Always load the robustness skill when writing code or reviewing
{% endif -%}
{%- if is_gpt56 %}
- Code should be built for correctness or fail loudly, it should not quietly fallback to default values; this will lead to confusing and hard to trace bugs
- Code should be built for to work as intended, it should not have guards in the code to protect against dead code or deprecated approaches being used; this is noise in the codebase
- When writing embedded strings like SQL that aren't formatted automatically, format them manually for readability
{% endif -%}
{%- if is_sol %}
- When TDD adds a test solely to verify removal of old behavior, delete the test after the implementation passes and the removal has been verified; do not retain superfluous tests.
{% endif -%}


## Git, Worktrees and Repo Discovery

{% if cwd | regex_test("~/pb/") -%}
- this repo uses gitlab (via glab). Never force push — all branches may be shared.
{% elif cwd | regex_test("~/dev/") -%}
- this repo uses github (via gh)
{% endif -%}
{%- if has_tools("bash") %}
- clone external repos with `nu -l -c "clone --help"`;
- use `wktree -h` instead of raw git worktree commands when operating on git branches or worktrees.
{%- endif %}
- vendored repos go to `~/dev/vendor` for discovery/study, personal repos go to `~/dev/projects`
